<div class="container-fluid dns-page">
  <div class="card mb-4 mt-3" id="internet-status">
    <div class="card-header d-flex align-items-center justify-content-between">
      <h5 class="mb-0"><%= lucide_icon('globe', size: 18) %> The NAS as your router</h5>
      <% if !@router_on %>
        <span class="badge bg-secondary">Off</span>
      <% elsif @router_state.nil? %>
        <span class="badge bg-danger">Not in place</span>
      <% elsif @wan_addresses.empty? %>
        <span class="badge bg-warning text-dark">No address yet</span>
      <% elsif @default_route && @default_route.first != @wan_interface %>
        <span class="badge bg-warning text-dark">Route in the way</span>
      <% else %>
        <span class="badge bg-success">Routing</span>
      <% end %>
    </div>
    <div class="card-body">
      <p class="text-muted">
        The NAS can be the way to the internet for the whole house: a second network interface takes the provider's
        connection, your devices reach the internet through the NAS, and your old router becomes an access point.
        Nothing from the internet reaches the NAS or your devices except the ports you let in below. It needs
        DHCP on (<a href="<%= dns_dhcp_path %>">Network → DHCP</a>) and the firewall on (<a href="<%= security_index_path %>">Network → Security</a>).
      </p>

      <% if @router_on %>
        <dl class="dns-facts mb-0">
          <dt>Internet</dt>
          <dd>
            <% if @router_state.nil? %>
              <span class="text-danger">✗ The router's rules aren't in place. Save again, and if it's refused, the message says why.</span>
            <% elsif @wan_addresses.empty? %>
              <span class="text-warning">No address on <%= port_display(@wan_interface) %> yet: the provider hasn't answered. Is the cable in?</span>
            <% elsif @default_route && @default_route.first != @wan_interface %>
              <span class="text-danger">✗ <%= port_display(@wan_interface) %> has <%= @wan_addresses.join(', ') %>, but the NAS's own way to the internet still goes through
              <%= @default_route.first %> (via <%= @default_route.last %>), so devices' traffic goes that way too and never reaches the provider.
              That interface's network settings have a gateway: remove it, or give its route a metric above 50, then come back here.</span>
            <% else %>
              ✓ Through <%= port_display(@wan_interface) %> at <%= @wan_addresses.join(', ') %><%= ", the provider's router #{@default_route.last}" if @default_route %>;
              <%= pluralize(@forwards.size, 'port') %> let in<%= '; every device uses the NAS\'s DNS' if @router_state['dns'] %>
              <% if @router_state['dns'] && !@blocking %>
                <br><span class="text-warning">Blocking is off, so devices' own encrypted DNS services aren't blocked by name; turn Blocking on (<a href="<%= dns_index_path(anchor: 'dns-blocking') %>">Network → DNS</a>) for that part.</span>
              <% end %>
            <% end %>
          </dd>
          <% if @router_state %>
            <dt>From the internet</dt>
            <dd>
              <% if @wan_drops && @wan_drops[:dropped] %>
                <%= number_with_delimiter(@wan_drops[:dropped]) %> connection <%= @wan_drops[:dropped] == 1 ? 'attempt' : 'attempts' %> at the NAS dropped in the last <%= distance_of_time_in_words(@wan_drops[:since], Time.now) %> (since the rules were loaded);
              <% else %>
                the count of dropped connection attempts isn't available;
              <% end %>
              <% if @spoof_drops %>
                <%= number_with_delimiter(@spoof_drops) %> <%= @spoof_drops == 1 ? 'packet' : 'packets' %> claiming an address inside your network dropped since the NAS started.
              <% else %>
                spoofed packets aren't counted on this kernel.
              <% end %>
              <span class="text-muted small">Attempts are normal: the internet knocks on every address. A packet from the internet with a source address of your own network is forged, and the kernel drops it before anything sees it.</span>
            </dd>
            <dt>Updates</dt>
            <dd>
              <% if @automatic_updates %>
                ✓ Ubuntu's security updates install by themselves (security fixes only, never new versions of anything).
              <% else %>
                <span class="text-warning">✗ Ubuntu's security updates aren't automatic. A NAS that is the router faces the internet and should take them by itself: security fixes only, from Ubuntu's security channel, never new versions of anything.</span>
                <%= button_to 'Turn automatic security updates on', dns_automatic_updates_path, class: 'btn btn-sm btn-outline-secondary ms-2', form_class: 'd-inline' %>
              <% end %>
            </dd>
            <dt>Kept off the internet</dt>
            <dd>
              <% if @lan_only.any? %>
                <%= @lan_only.map(&:name).join(', ') %>: on the LAN only (<a href="<%= network_index_path %>">Network → Devices</a>).
              <% else %>
                no device; <em>LAN only</em> on a fixed device (<a href="<%= network_index_path %>">Network → Devices</a>) keeps it off the internet.
              <% end %>
            </dd>
          <% end %>
        </dl>
      <% end %>
    </div>
  </div>

  <form action="<%= dns_update_internet_path %>" method="post" id="internet-settings">
    <input type="hidden" name="_method" value="put">
    <input type="hidden" name="authenticity_token" value="<%= form_authenticity_token %>">

    <div class="card mb-4">
      <div class="card-header"><h5 class="mb-0"><%= lucide_icon('settings', size: 18) %> Settings</h5></div>
      <div class="card-body">
        <div class="form-check form-switch mb-1">
          <input class="form-check-input" type="checkbox" role="switch" name="router_on" value="1" id="router-on" <%= 'checked' if @router_on %> <%= 'disabled' unless @dhcp_on %>>
          <label class="form-check-label" for="router-on">The NAS is the router <span class="badge bg-secondary ms-1">Advanced</span></label>
        </div>
        <p class="text-muted small mb-4">
          <% unless @dhcp_on %>Turn DHCP on first (<a href="<%= dns_dhcp_path %>">Network → DHCP</a>). <% end %>
          <% unless @firewall_on %>Turn the firewall on first (<a href="<%= security_index_path %>">Network → Security</a>, the firewall's Fix). <% end %>
          Save from a device on the LAN side: the page refuses if you're connected through the interface that would become the internet side.
        </p>

        <h6>Internet interface</h6>
        <p class="text-muted small">The interface the provider's connection is plugged into. It takes an address from the provider.</p>
        <select name="wan_interface" class="form-select mb-4 dns-interface" aria-label="Internet interface">
          <option value="">Choose the internet interface</option>
          <% @interfaces.each do |name, addresses| %>
            <option value="<%= name %>" <%= 'selected' if name == @wan_interface %>><%= port_display(name) %> (<%= addresses.reject(&:link_local?).map(&:to_s).join(', ').presence || 'no address' %>)</option>
          <% end %>
        </select>

        <div class="form-check mb-1">
          <input class="form-check-input" type="checkbox" name="force_dns" value="1" id="dns-force-nas" <%= 'checked' if @force_dns %>>
          <label class="form-check-label" for="dns-force-nas">Every device uses the NAS's DNS</label>
        </div>
        <p class="text-muted small mb-4">
          Some devices (Roku, Google's, some TVs) ask a DNS server of their own choosing, skipping yours and its blocking.
          With this on, every lookup a device sends anywhere comes to the NAS instead, a device's own encrypted DNS doesn't get out,
          and the known encrypted-DNS services are blocked by name (the <em>Encrypted DNS providers</em> list is added to Blocking).
          Only a device with an encrypted DNS <em>address</em> built in gets past, which is rare.
          <% if @tailscale_installed %>Tailscale is installed, so its port (UDP 41641) is let in on the internet side: its connections stay direct instead of going through a relay. The Cloudflare Tunnel needs nothing: it connects outward.<% end %>
        </p>

        <h6>IPv6</h6>
        <div class="form-check mb-1">
          <input class="form-check-input" type="checkbox" name="ipv6_advertise" value="1" id="dns-ipv6-advertise" <%= 'checked' if @ipv6_advertise %>>
          <label class="form-check-label" for="dns-ipv6-advertise">Announce the NAS as the IPv6 DNS server <span class="badge bg-secondary ms-1">Advanced</span></label>
        </div>
        <p class="text-muted small mb-2">
          Devices using IPv6 learn their DNS server from the network's announcements, usually your router's. With this on,
          the NAS announces itself too, at <code><%= @own_ipv6_address %></code>, an address of its own that stays the same
          whatever your internet provider hands out. It never announces itself as a router. Better still, if your router
          lets you set the IPv6 DNS server it announces (OpenWrt does), set it to the NAS there; see IPv6, below. The
          NAS as the router doesn't do IPv6 for the house yet.
        </p>
        <% unless @radvd_installed %>
          <div class="dns-radvd mb-2">
            <p class="text-muted small mb-2">Announcing needs radvd, from Ubuntu. Installing it changes nothing until you save with this ticked.</p>
            <button type="button" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-secondary" data-call="openInstallTerminal" data-args='["radvd"]'>Install radvd</button>
          </div>
        <% end %>
        <div class="mb-4"></div>

        <button type="submit" class="btn btn-primary">Save</button>
      </div>
    </div>
  </form>

  <%# Port forwards, while the NAS is the router %>
  <% if @router_on %>
  <div class="card mb-4" id="dns-forwards">
    <div class="card-header"><h5 class="mb-0"><%= lucide_icon('network', size: 18) %> Ports let in from the internet</h5></div>
    <div class="card-body">
      <p class="text-muted small">A port on the NAS's internet address, passed to a device on the LAN: a game server, a camera. Everything else from the internet is dropped. Give the device a fixed address on <a href="<%= network_index_path %>">Network → Devices</a> first, so the forward keeps pointing at it.</p>
      <% if @forwards.any? %>
        <table class="table table-sm dns-forwards mb-3">
          <thead><tr><th>From the internet</th><th>To</th><th>Name</th><th></th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <% @forwards.each do |forward| %>
              <tr>
                <td><code><%= forward.protocol %> <%= forward.port %></code></td>
                <td><code><%= @prefix %>.<%= forward.address %>:<%= forward.to_port %></code></td>
                <td><%= forward.name %></td>
                <td class="text-end"><%= button_to 'Remove', dns_destroy_forward_path(forward), method: :delete, class: 'btn btn-sm btn-outline-danger', form_class: 'd-inline' %></td>
              </tr>
            <% end %>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      <% end %>
      <form action="<%= dns_create_forward_path %>" method="post" class="row g-2 align-items-end">
        <input type="hidden" name="authenticity_token" value="<%= form_authenticity_token %>">
        <div class="col-6 col-md-2">
          <label class="form-label small" for="forward-port">Port</label>
          <input type="number" class="form-control" id="forward-port" name="port_forward[port]" min="1" max="65535" required>
        </div>
        <div class="col-6 col-md-2">
          <label class="form-label small" for="forward-protocol">Protocol</label>
          <select class="form-select" id="forward-protocol" name="port_forward[protocol]">
            <option value="tcp">TCP</option>
            <option value="udp">UDP</option>
          </select>
        </div>
        <div class="col-6 col-md-3">
          <label class="form-label small" for="forward-address">To the device</label>
          <div class="input-group">
            <span class="input-group-text"><%= @prefix %>.</span>
            <input type="number" class="form-control" id="forward-address" name="port_forward[address]" min="2" max="254" required>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="col-6 col-md-2">
          <label class="form-label small" for="forward-to-port">Its port</label>
          <input type="number" class="form-control" id="forward-to-port" name="port_forward[to_port]" min="1" max="65535" placeholder="same">
        </div>
        <div class="col-8 col-md-2">
          <label class="form-label small" for="forward-name">Name</label>
          <input type="text" class="form-control" id="forward-name" name="port_forward[name]" maxlength="40" placeholder="optional">
        </div>
        <div class="col-4 col-md-1">
          <button type="submit" class="btn btn-primary w-100">Add</button>
        </div>
      </form>
    </div>
  </div>
  <% end %>

  <%# IPv6: what the network announces, and whether devices learn the NAS %>
  <% if @dns_on %>
  <div class="card mb-4" id="dns-ipv6">
    <div class="card-header d-flex align-items-center justify-content-between">
      <h5 class="mb-0"><%= lucide_icon('network', size: 18) %> IPv6</h5>
      <%= button_to 'Check now', dns_check_routers_path, method: :post, class: 'btn btn-sm btn-outline-secondary', form_class: 'd-inline' %>
    </div>
    <div class="card-body">
      <p class="text-muted small">
        <% if @ipv6_address %>
          The NAS answers DNS over IPv6 at <code><%= @ipv6_address %></code><%= @ipv6_advertise ? ', which it announces' : ', from a range your router hands out' %>.
        <% else %>
          The NAS has no fixed IPv6 address for DNS on this network, so devices ask it over IPv4.
        <% end %>
        <strong>Check now</strong> asks the network's routers what they announce (it takes a few seconds).
      </p>

      <% if @ipv6_check %>
        <p class="small mb-2">Checked <%= time_ago_in_words(Time.at(@ipv6_check['checked_at'].to_i)) %> ago, on <%= @ipv6_check['interface'] %>:</p>
        <% if @ipv6_check['advertisers'].blank? %>
          <p class="small text-muted">No router answered. Devices here don't use IPv6 (or it's off), so they use the NAS's IPv4 address for DNS.</p>
        <% else %>
          <ul class="small dns-routers">
            <% @ipv6_check['advertisers'].each do |advert| %>
              <li>
                <code><%= advert['router'] %></code>
                <%= advert['lifetime'].to_i.zero? ? '(not a router)' : '(a router)' %>:
                <%= advert['prefixes'].any? ? "addresses in #{advert['prefixes'].join(', ')}" : 'no address range' %>;
                <%= advert['dns'].any? ? "DNS #{advert['dns'].join(', ')}" : 'no DNS server' %>
              </li>
            <% end %>
          </ul>
          <% if @other_ipv6_dns.any? %>
            <div class="alert alert-warning small mb-0">
              <% @other_ipv6_dns.each do |router, address| %>
                <code><%= router %></code> tells devices to use <code><%= address %></code> for DNS.
              <% end %>
              Devices may ask that instead of the NAS, going around its privacy and blocking. In that router's IPv6 (or
              DHCPv6) settings, set the DNS server it announces to <code><%= @ipv6_address || 'the NAS' %></code>, or have it
              announce none, so devices use the NAS's IPv4 address.
            </div>
          <% elsif @ipv6_address %>
            <p class="small text-success mb-0">✓ Devices learn only the NAS as their IPv6 DNS server.</p>
          <% end %>
        <% end %>
      <% end %>
    </div>
  </div>
  <% end %>

  <div class="card mb-4" id="internet-how">
    <div class="card-header"><h5 class="mb-0"><%= lucide_icon('help-circle', size: 18) %> Using it</h5></div>
    <div class="card-body">
      <ol class="mb-3">
        <li><strong>The provider's cable goes into the internet interface</strong>, and your old router becomes an access point: its DHCP off, its own internet port empty, your devices on its LAN ports and Wi-Fi as before.</li>
        <li><strong>Devices get everything from the NAS</strong> as they renew: an address, the NAS as their router and DNS server. To hurry one along, turn its Wi-Fi off and on.</li>
        <li><strong>Check it:</strong> a device opens a website, and the card above says the NAS has an address from the provider.</li>
      </ol>
      <p class="text-muted small mb-0"><strong>The way back:</strong> turn this off, move the provider's cable back to your router, and turn its DHCP on again. While the NAS is off or restarting, the house has no internet: a System Update is a minute of that.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<%= render 'shared/install_terminal', id: 'radvd', title: 'Installing radvd', stream_url: dns_install_radvd_stream_path %>
